<h1>Skill Format</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Plain Markdown, a deliberately small frontmatter parser, and section-level search.</p>

<h2 id="files">One flat file per skill</h2>
<p>The current store reads only top-level <code>.md</code> files from <code>&lt;home&gt;/skills/</code>. It does not recursively index pack subdirectories.</p>
<div class="code-block"><span class="dim">~/.mcp-librarian/</span>
<span class="dim">  skills/</span>
<span class="dim">    api-design.md</span>
<span class="dim">    security-hardening.md</span></div>
<p>If an older installation uses <code>skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</code>, run <code>node bin/migrate.js</code>. The migration writes flat copies and intentionally leaves old directories in place.</p>

<h2 id="frontmatter">Recognized frontmatter</h2>
<p>The runtime does not use a general YAML library. It splits the opening <code>---</code> block into lines at the first colon and recognizes these fields:</p>
<table class="cmd-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Runtime status</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Required by <code>validate_skill</code>/<code>create_skill</code></td><td>Skill identity and search metadata.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>version</code></td><td>Required by validation</td><td>Returned as metadata. Runtime pack deduplication does not compare versions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>category</code></td><td>Required by validation</td><td>Inline bracket list used in metadata search.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>description</code></td><td>Required by validation</td><td>Returned and indexed as metadata.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>sources</code></td><td>Optional</td><td>Inline bracket list used by the heuristic quality score.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<div class="code-block"><span class="dim">---</span>
<span class="kw">name</span>: api-design
<span class="kw">version</span>: 1.0.0
<span class="kw">category</span>: [api, backend]
<span class="kw">description</span>: Patterns for an HTTP API
<span class="kw">sources</span>: [https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457.html]
<span class="dim">---</span>

<span class="cmd">## Error responses</span>

Use one stable error shape.

<span class="cmd">### Problem details</span>

Return an appropriate media type and status code.</div>

<p>Fields such as singular <code>source</code>, <code>tags</code>, <code>author</code>, and <code>requires</code> are not read by the current runtime. They may remain in Markdown as human metadata, but they do not affect retrieval or dependency loading.</p>
<div class="warning-box"><strong>YAML-lite means literal constraints.</strong> Use the inline bracket form for <code>category</code> and <code>sources</code>. Multiline YAML arrays are not parsed as arrays by the current store.</div>

<h2 id="validation">What validation checks</h2>
<p><code>validate_skill</code> and <code>create_skill</code> check:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The content begins with a <code>---</code>-delimited frontmatter block.</li>
  <li>The four required field names appear in that block.</li>
  <li>At least one line begins with <code>## </code>.</li>
  <li>The prose passes the bundled content-guard regexes.</li>
</ul>
<p>They do not fully parse YAML, enforce semantic versions, verify URLs, fact-check the body, enforce a description length, or validate optional fields. Runtime <code>install_pack</code> applies the content guard and path/dedup logic but does not call this full validation helper for each file.</p>

<h2 id="filenames">create_skill filenames</h2>
<p><code>create_skill</code> requires a flat filename ending in <code>.md</code>. It must start with an ASCII letter or digit; remaining characters may be letters, digits, dot, dash, or underscore. Slashes are rejected.</p>
<div class="code-block">create_skill({
  <span class="kw">filename</span>: <span class="str">"api-design.md"</span>,
  <span class="kw">content</span>: <span class="str">"complete Markdown string"</span>
})</div>
<p>The response's <code>skill</code> value comes from the frontmatter <code>name</code>, falling back to the filename stem.</p>

<h2 id="chunking">How sections become search chunks</h2>
<p>The BM25 content index starts at <code>##</code> headings:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A <code>##</code> section with body text and no <code>###</code> child becomes one chunk.</li>
  <li>Text between a <code>##</code> heading and its first <code>###</code> becomes a parent chunk only when it contains body text.</li>
  <li>Each <code>###</code> subsection becomes its own child chunk.</li>
  <li>Text before the first <code>##</code>, including an <code>#</code> title, is not a body chunk.</li>
</ul>
<p>Slugs are lowercase heading text with spaces changed to hyphens and non-alphanumeric characters removed. The example produces <code>error-responses</code> and <code>error-responses/problem-details</code>.</p>

<p>Each chunk includes its heading in its ordinary text. Headings do not receive a separate BM25 boost. Names, descriptions, and categories are indexed in a separate metadata chunk.</p>

<h2 id="retrieval">Sections versus full files</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>find_skill</code> returns matching chunks, their slugs, snippets, and scores.</li>
  <li><code>load_section</code> reads the file and returns one exact slug.</li>
  <li><code>load_skill</code> returns the full file and all slugs.</li>
  <li><code>find_and_load</code> uses search to choose a skill but returns that skill's full file.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="ranking">Quality-weighted ranking</h2>
<p>With quality weighting enabled (the default), search over-fetches BM25 candidates, normalizes each raw score against the best candidate, then blends relevance with a per-skill heuristic. The default blend uses <code>qualityWeight: 0.4</code>.</p>
<p>The skill-quality heuristic combines:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Token shapes treated as specificity signals.</li>
  <li>Fenced blocks and inline code as example signals.</li>
  <li>Numbered steps, a small imperative-verb list, and shell fences as actionability signals.</li>
  <li>The maximum recognized reputation score from optional <code>sources</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Content signals make up 70% of that skill-quality score and source reputation 30%. This is a formatting/domain heuristic, not an evaluation of truth, security, or usefulness. Disable it with <code>qualityWeighting: false</code> in <code>config.json</code> for pure BM25.</p>

<h2 id="duplicates">Names and duplicate files</h2>
<p>If multiple flat files have the same frontmatter <code>name</code>, the newest modification time wins during store load. Pack deduplication first skips an exact content-hash match; otherwise, the same frontmatter name is treated as an update regardless of the <code>version</code> field.</p>
